Yes. I’d make v0 almost deliberately boring: **prove registration, lifecycle observation, control acquisition, and bounded telemetry before introducing any interception at all**.

The important distinction to expose is:

```text
REGISTERED → INSTALLING → INSTALLED → ACTIVATING → ACTIVATED → CONTROLLING PAGE
```

`active` and `navigator.serviceWorker.controller !== null` are related, but not identical. For the later fault lab, **“controlling this page” is the state that really matters**.

I’d start with an in-memory ring buffer on the page side, fed by registration events plus a tiny message protocol from the worker.

```ts
// service-worker-log.ts

export type ServiceWorkerLogLevel = "debug" | "info" | "warn" | "error";

export interface ServiceWorkerLogEntry {
  timestamp: string;
  source: "page" | "service-worker";
  level: ServiceWorkerLogLevel;
  event: string;
  detail?: string;
}

export class ServiceWorkerLogBuffer {
  private readonly entries: ServiceWorkerLogEntry[] = [];

  public constructor(private readonly capacity = 200) {
    if (capacity < 1) {
      throw new Error("Log buffer capacity must be at least 1");
    }
  }

  public add(entry: Omit<ServiceWorkerLogEntry, "timestamp">): void {
    this.entries.push({
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      ...entry,
    });

    if (this.entries.length > this.capacity) {
      this.entries.splice(0, this.entries.length - this.capacity);
    }
  }

  public snapshot(): readonly ServiceWorkerLogEntry[] {
    return [...this.entries];
  }

  public clear(): void {
    this.entries.length = 0;
  }
}
```

Then registration:

```ts
// service-worker-client.ts

import {
  ServiceWorkerLogBuffer,
  type ServiceWorkerLogEntry,
} from "./service-worker-log";

interface ServiceWorkerMessage {
  type: "sw:log";
  entry: {
    level: ServiceWorkerLogEntry["level"];
    event: string;
    detail?: string;
  };
}

export const serviceWorkerLog = new ServiceWorkerLogBuffer(200);

function log(
  level: ServiceWorkerLogEntry["level"],
  event: string,
  detail?: string,
): void {
  serviceWorkerLog.add({
    source: "page",
    level,
    event,
    detail,
  });

  console[level](`[service-worker] ${event}`, detail ?? "");
}

function observeWorker(
  label: "installing" | "waiting" | "active",
  worker: ServiceWorker | null,
): void {
  if (!worker) {
    return;
  }

  log("info", `worker:${label}`, worker.state);

  worker.addEventListener("statechange", () => {
    log("info", `worker:${label}:statechange`, worker.state);
  });
}

export async function registerServiceWorker(): Promise<void> {
  if (!("serviceWorker" in navigator)) {
    log("warn", "unsupported");
    return;
  }

  navigator.serviceWorker.addEventListener("controllerchange", () => {
    const controller = navigator.serviceWorker.controller;

    log(
      "info",
      "controllerchange",
      controller
        ? `controller=${controller.scriptURL}`
        : "controller=null",
    );
  });

  navigator.serviceWorker.addEventListener("message", (event: MessageEvent) => {
    const message = event.data as ServiceWorkerMessage;

    if (
      !message ||
      message.type !== "sw:log" ||
      typeof message.entry?.event !== "string"
    ) {
      return;
    }

    serviceWorkerLog.add({
      source: "service-worker",
      level: message.entry.level,
      event: message.entry.event,
      detail: message.entry.detail,
    });
  });

  try {
    log("info", "register:start");

    const registration = await navigator.serviceWorker.register(
      "/service-worker.js",
      {
        scope: "/",
        type: "module",
      },
    );

    log("info", "register:complete", `scope=${registration.scope}`);

    observeWorker("installing", registration.installing);
    observeWorker("waiting", registration.waiting);
    observeWorker("active", registration.active);

    registration.addEventListener("updatefound", () => {
      log("info", "updatefound");

      observeWorker("installing", registration.installing);
    });

    const ready = await navigator.serviceWorker.ready;

    log(
      "info",
      "ready",
      ready.active
        ? `active=${ready.active.scriptURL}`
        : "active worker unavailable",
    );

    const controller = navigator.serviceWorker.controller;

    log(
      "info",
      "controller",
      controller
        ? `controlled by ${controller.scriptURL}`
        : "page is not currently controlled",
    );
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    const detail =
      error instanceof Error
        ? `${error.name}: ${error.message}`
        : String(error);

    log("error", "register:failed", detail);
  }
}
```

And the worker itself can initially do almost nothing:

```ts
// service-worker.ts

declare const self: ServiceWorkerGlobalScope;

interface WorkerLogMessage {
  type: "sw:log";
  entry: {
    level: "debug" | "info" | "warn" | "error";
    event: string;
    detail?: string;
  };
}

async function broadcast(
  event: string,
  detail?: string,
): Promise<void> {
  const clients = await self.clients.matchAll({
    type: "window",
    includeUncontrolled: true,
  });

  const message: WorkerLogMessage = {
    type: "sw:log",
    entry: {
      level: "info",
      event,
      detail,
    },
  };

  for (const client of clients) {
    client.postMessage(message);
  }
}

self.addEventListener("install", (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    broadcast("install"),
  );
});

self.addEventListener("activate", (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    (async () => {
      await broadcast("activate:start");

      await self.clients.claim();

      await broadcast("activate:complete");
    })(),
  );
});
```

One subtlety: the `install` broadcast isn't guaranteed to reach anything useful on first installation, because the page is not yet controlled and lifecycle timing is awkward. That's why I’d treat the **page's observation of `worker.state` as authoritative for lifecycle**, and SW-originated messages as supplementary telemetry.

I would also **not use `skipWaiting()` yet**. `clients.claim()` is useful because it lets you observe the first page becoming controlled without forcing a reload, but `skipWaiting()` affects upgrade semantics and is worth choosing deliberately once you start thinking about worker updates.

The first UI panel could therefore be nothing more than:

```text
SERVICE WORKER

Registration    ● REGISTERED
Lifecycle       ● ACTIVATED
Page control    ● CONTROLLED
Scope           /
Script          /service-worker.js

EVENT LOG
23:42:01.143  register:start
23:42:01.161  register:complete
23:42:01.163  worker:installing
23:42:01.170  statechange → installed
23:42:01.171  statechange → activating
23:42:01.175  activate:start
23:42:01.178  statechange → activated
23:42:01.180  controllerchange
23:42:01.181  activate:complete
```

That alone gives you the foundation for everything later:

```text
registration
    ↓
lifecycle observation
    ↓
control detection
    ↓
message protocol
    ↓
bounded event buffer
    ↓
request telemetry
    ↓
fault rules
```

I'd actually resist adding `fetch` handling at all in this first version. Once this panel reliably shows lifecycle transitions across first visit, reload, update, multiple tabs, and worker replacement, then add a completely passive `fetch` observer before allowing it to modify responses.

That progression makes debugging the eventual fault lab much easier because you'll know whether a weird result is **SW lifecycle**, **message transport**, or **fault-engine behaviour** rather than debugging all three simultaneously.

**Confidence: 99%.** This is the boundary I'd start with.
